+# Agent Note: CLI provides the app-boot group peer
+
+Status: implemented
+
+English | [中文](2026-08-14-cli-provides-app-boot-group-peer.zh.md)
+
+## Problem
+
+`@deepseek-ai/dsh-app-boot` imports `@deepseek-ai/cordis-plugin-group` and declares it as a required peer dependency. `@deepseek-ai/dsh` depends on app-boot but did not provide that peer. The workspace development install supplied the package elsewhere and allowed source tests to pass, while `pnpm --filter @deepseek-ai/dsh --prod deploy --legacy` omitted it. Starting the deployed `lib/bin.js` then failed with `ERR_MODULE_NOT_FOUND` before the web server printed its address.
+
+## Decision
+
+The CLI package declares `@deepseek-ai/cordis-plugin-group` as a production dependency. A production deployment therefore includes the peer that app-boot imports during startup without depending on workspace development dependencies or a packaging wrapper.
+
+The CLI manifest test records this host responsibility directly. Assembled-product verification must also start the production deployment with its packaged Node executable because workspace module resolution cannot prove the deployed dependency graph.
+
+## Alternatives considered
+
+- **Injecting group only in desktop packaging.** This would leave the published CLI package and every other production deploy command broken, so the package manifest owns the dependency instead.
+- **Moving group from app-boot peers into app-boot dependencies.** App-boot uses peers so each host owns the Cordis plugin instances in its composition. Changing that ownership for every host is broader than correcting the CLI host.
+- **Relying on another CLI dependency to install group transitively.** Peer resolution must not depend on an unrelated package retaining an implementation detail, and the current production deploy demonstrates that no such guarantee exists.
+
+## Consequences
+
+Production CLI deployments contain one additional vendored Cordis plugin and can load app-boot before any profile configuration is read. The CLI manifest has a small explicit dependency cost, while other app-boot hosts retain their existing peer ownership.
+
+## Testing
+
+`apps/cli/tests/production-dependencies.spec.ts` checks the CLI manifest. Desktop release verification deploys production dependencies, starts `lib/bin.js web --host 127.0.0.1 --port 0` with the packaged Node executable, and requires the loopback startup address before signing the distributable.
